WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.770
I think there's a line in my college
application essay that goes, "If math and

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:10.790
science hadn't found me, my mother always
says I'd be a Broadway star."

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:15.770
The MTA Playwrights Lab is the sort of first
ever of its kind here at MIT,

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:20.550
a collaboration between playwriting
students and theatre professionals. So I

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.300
took eight playwrights into my
playwrights workshop this spring and

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.390
each of those eight writers is getting
a professional staged reading this

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.920
weekend as part of the Lab.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.340
Seeing the
process of a play going from an idea to

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.380
something that might be executable to
something that might be publishable

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.260
has been a great experience.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.880
I could become a Galactic Arboriterator, or start a business.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.380
How are you gonna become an arbitrator when you can't even say the word?

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.320
I'll learn.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.140
The first
two readings, both of them sold out, so we

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.100
had over fifty students here in the
audience, and it's mostly students, so

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.420
it's exciting that a lot of young MIT kids
who have probably never seen a staged

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.340
reading before, come in here to support
their fellow students' works.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.420
And being inspired by science, because my play, as
you just saw, is like science fiction, being

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.070
inspired by science and, like, wanting so
much from the world and always

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.509
questioning, like , as we technologically
advance so much, are we, like, morally

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:19.680
advancing, are we doing anything that's
like --- are we on par with the things that we're creating?

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.340
Is it not simpler and easier to get your money another way?

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.740
Well, yes, but you were opposed to my previous plan.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.160
And what makes you think I won't be opposed to this one?

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:34.360
Because I expect the money from Port Saba to sing to you. Ooh yes, look at that...

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.060
The rules of storytelling, which is
really what playwrights are, you know,

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.640
essentially learning, is really going to
be useful whether or not it's about

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:47.430
making a presentation or writing an
essay or building a website. I think that,

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.270
like, whatever you're doing, you're kind
of telling a story in some way, and so if

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.729
you learn how stories are made, and you
learn what some of the sort of

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:00.240
interlocking parts of stories are, even
if you're just learning the rules so as

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.750
to break them, I think it's kind of good
to sort of think about what you're doing

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.920
as an act of storytelling.

